      <p class="section-subtitle">LLMs are being explored across a wide spectrum of medical tasks</p>

      <div class="app-item">
        <div class="app-icon-box">&#x270E;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Clinical Documentation <span class="maturity-tag tag-pilot">Pilot Deployments</span></h3>
          <p>Automated note generation, discharge summaries, and EHR integration. Several health systems have integrated LLM-powered assistants into electronic health records.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="app-item">
        <div class="app-icon-box">&#x2316;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Diagnostic Support <span class="maturity-tag tag-research">Research Stage</span></h3>
          <p>Differential diagnosis generation, radiology report interpretation, pathology report analysis, and clinical decision support systems.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="app-item">
        <div class="app-icon-box">&#x2709;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Patient Communication <span class="maturity-tag tag-adoption">Early Adoption</span></h3>
          <p>Answering patient questions, simplifying medical jargon, generating after-visit summaries, and multilingual health communication.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="app-item">
        <div class="app-icon-box">&#x2666;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Medical Education <span class="maturity-tag tag-active">Active Use</span></h3>
          <p>USMLE and board exam preparation, case-based learning, clinical reasoning tutoring, and personalized study materials.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="app-item">
        <div class="app-icon-box">&#x2317;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Biomedical Research <span class="maturity-tag tag-emerging">Emerging</span></h3>
          <p>Literature synthesis, hypothesis generation, clinical trial protocol drafting, and automated systematic review assistance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-col">
      <h2><span class="accent-coral">Risks, Limitations,</span> &amp; Ethical Concerns</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle">Despite impressive benchmarks, significant concerns persist</p>

      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon-box">&#x26A0;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Hallucinations &amp; Factual Errors</h3>
          <p>LLMs can fabricate plausible-sounding but incorrect medical information. Studies report hallucination rates of up to 30% in medical QA tasks, including fake citations and erroneous clinical recommendations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon-box">&#x2260;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Bias &amp; Health Equity</h3>
          <p>Models may perpetuate or amplify biases present in training data, leading to disparities in care recommendations across demographic groups — race, gender, socioeconomic status, and geography.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon-box">&#x1F512;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Patient Privacy &amp; Data Security</h3>
          <p>Patient data used in prompts may be transmitted to external servers, raising serious HIPAA and GDPR compliance concerns. De-identification remains unreliable with LLMs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon-box">&#x2718;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Lack of Clinical Validation</h3>
          <p>Most LLM evaluations rely on multiple-choice benchmarks rather than prospective clinical trials. Performance on real-world clinical scenarios remains poorly characterized.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="risk-item">
        <div class="risk-icon-box">&#x26D4;</div>
        <div class="item-content">
          <h3>Automation Bias &amp; Over-Reliance</h3>
          <p>Clinicians may develop automation bias, accepting LLM outputs without sufficient critical appraisal. This risk is compounded by the authoritative tone of LLM-generated text.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PERFORMANCE BENCHMARKS -->
  <div class="perf-section">
    <h2>Performance Benchmarks &amp; Evidence</h2>
    <div class="perf-cards">
      <div class="perf-card">
        <div class="model-name">GPT-4</div>
        <div class="model-score">&gt;90%</div>
        <div class="model-desc">Scored above 90% on all three USMLE steps, substantially exceeding the ~60% passing threshold. Demonstrates strong medical knowledge encoding.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="perf-card">
        <div class="model-name">Med-PaLM 2</div>
        <div class="model-score">86.5%</div>
        <div class="model-desc">Google's medical LLM achieved 86.5% on MedQA benchmark, approaching expert physician-level performance on clinical reasoning tasks.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="perf-card">
        <div class="model-name">GPT-3.5 (Baseline)</div>
        <div class="model-score">~60%</div>
        <div class="model-desc">Earlier generation model scored near the USMLE passing threshold but fell short on complex clinical reasoning and multi-step diagnostic tasks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="perf-note">
      <strong>Important:</strong> These benchmark results, while impressive, reflect performance on standardized multiple-choice examinations. Real-world clinical deployment requires prospective validation in diverse healthcare settings with actual patient outcomes.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- FRAMEWORK -->
  <div class="framework-section">
    <h2>A Framework for Responsible Deployment</h2>
    <p class="fw-subtitle">Multi-stakeholder recommendations for integrating LLMs safely into clinical workflows</p>
    <div class="fw-steps">
      <div class="fw-step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <h4>Prospective Evaluation</h4>
        <p>Rigorous testing in diverse clinical settings before deployment, with real patient outcomes as endpoints</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fw-step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <h4>Human-in-the-Loop</h4>
        <p>Mandatory clinician oversight of all LLM-generated outputs; AI as augmentation, not replacement</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fw-step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <h4>Transparent Reporting</h4>
        <p>Clear disclosure of model limitations, training data sources, and known failure modes to end users</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fw-step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <h4>Equity Audits</h4>
        <p>Systematic assessment of differential performance across patient populations to prevent disparities</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fw-step">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <h4>Regulatory Alignment</h4>
        <p>Compliance with evolving FDA, EMA, and WHO guidance on AI-based medical devices and SaMD</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- FOOTER -->
  <div class="footer">
    <span>Source: Nature Medicine (July 2023) — A landmark review of LLMs in medicine</span>
    <span>Medical AI &amp; Digital Health Conference 2024</span>
  </div>

</div>
</body>
</html>
